# Use Native with MediaPipe & Template Matching

## Context and Problem Statement
We are building a hybrid Flutter application to control a robot via real-time hand gestures. The system requires high-performance tracking and the ability for users to define custom gestures at runtime. Existing solutions on `pub.dev` were found to be outdated or incompatible, and processing video frames directly in Dart caused performance bottlenecks.

## Decision Drivers
* High-performance video processing
* Requirement User-defined gestures
* Stability
* Precision

## Considered Options
* Native + MediaPipe
* Existing Flutter Packages (`pub.dev`)
* Pure Dart Implementation
* Cloud-Based Processing
* Google ML Kit

## Decision Outcome
Chosen option: **Native + MediaPipe**, because it offloads heavy image processing to the Native thread to keep the UI smooth, and allows us to implement custom "Template Matching" logic that existing plugins do not support.

### Consequences
* Good: High performance (No UI lag)
* Good: Users can teach new gestures instantly
* Bad: Increased complexity (requires Kotlin/Swift & Dart code)

## Other Options

### Existing Flutter Packages
* Good: Fast initial implementation
* Bad: Many packages on `pub.dev` are outdated or unmaintained
* Bad: Lack internal access to insert custom "Template Matching" logic

### Pure Dart Implementation
* Good: Single codebase (Dart only)
* Bad: Massive performance cost transferring video frames across the bridge

### Cloud-Based Processing
* Good: Powerful inference capabilities
* Bad: Network latency makes real-time bot control impossible

### Google ML Kit
* Good: Simple API integration
* Bad: Less detailed hand skeleton compared to MediaPipe